{"answer":"The critic's central contradiction claim fails on the target's own terms: ABSTAIN roots do not participate in attack evaluation, so an ABSTAIN node has no attackers whose labels could force it to 'out' in phase two. Re-evaluating ABSTAIN nodes against resulting labels does not entail applying defeat rules that would mark them 'out' when their defenders are gone; 're-evaluate' here means resolving the ABSTAIN (pending) status given the fixed context, not rerunning the grounded operator's attack/defense logic on them. The critic conflates support-loss with defeat, but the target never states that a node becomes 'out' merely because its defenders are 'out'—that is an imported assumption about argumentation semantics, not a clause of the target. The committed-root validity guarantee is therefore not contradicted. The critic's determinism objection is also not established: the target states the second phase is not a monotone operator on the full lattice, which is offered as a candid observation, not a concession that determinism fails. A non-monotone second phase does not entail non-determinism; determinism can still be guaranteed by the fixed phase ordering itself. The critic's leap from 'not monotone' to 'determinism not preserved' is unsupported. Both clauses of the critic's case rest on inferences the target does not state, so neither contradiction nor determinism-loss is established."}